---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import ToastBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/ToastBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="toast-attr-duration">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A toast lives for a few seconds, then leaves. That usually means a <code
        >setTimeout</code
      > per toast, and pausing it on hover means clearing the timer, tracking how
      much time is left and starting a new one.
    </p>
    <p>
      CSS animations already have a clock that pauses. What they couldn't do was
      read the duration from the element. Not anymore.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Three animations in a row: enter, hold and exit. <code>hold</code> changes
        nothing, it only takes time
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>attr(data-duration type(&lt;time&gt;), 5s)</code> reads the lifetime
        from HTML, with <code>5s</code> when the attribute is missing or isn't a time
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>animation-play-state: paused</code> on hover and focus stops the clock
      </li>
      <li>
        JS removes the toast on <code>animationend</code> of the exit animation, no
        timers
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.toast {
  animation:
    toast-enter var(--enter) both,
    toast-hold attr(data-duration type(<time>), 5s) linear both,
    toast-exit var(--exit) both;
  animation-delay:
    0s,
    var(--enter),
    calc(var(--enter) + attr(data-duration type(<time>), 5s));
}

.toast:hover,
.toast:focus-within {
  animation-play-state: paused;
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <ToastBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>animation</code> and <code>animation-delay</code> are declared twice:
        first with a fixed <code>5s</code>, then with <code>attr()</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        Browsers without typed <code>attr()</code> drop the second declaration and
        keep the first
      </li>
      <li>
        Every toast then lasts 5 seconds and ignores <code>data-duration</code>,
        but it still pauses and still leaves
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
